WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.260
From a bike ride with an alien to&nbsp;
a father-son lightsaber battle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:09.656
these sci-fi scenes are fantastical,&nbsp;
unforgettable, and infinitely rewatchable.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:14.280
The Death Star attack and trench run in "Star&nbsp;
Wars: A New Hope" is one of the most iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:18.840
sequences in the history of film. It's arguably&nbsp;
the most famous sequence in the entire "Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.860
Wars" saga, and it set the tone at the very&nbsp;
beginning for how a "Star Wars" space battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:28.260
should look and feel. George Lucas and his team&nbsp;
famously drew inspiration from dog fights in World&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:33.480
War II dramas, and that influence is palpable.&nbsp;
The whole battle takes place in the open vacuum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:38.940
of space, yet every shot-down fighter and close&nbsp;
engagement is packed with tight, frenetic energy.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:43.560
Narratively, this scene is just as iconic.&nbsp;
From Luke turning off his targeting computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:47.880
for the impossible shot to Han flying in at&nbsp;
the last second to save his friend's skin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.540
it's full of tropes in the best&nbsp;
way. John Williams' triumphant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.720
score booms in the background,&nbsp;
and the Death Star does the same.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.721
"I knew you'd come back, I just knew it!"

00:00:56.721 --> 00:00:59.443
"Well, I wasn't going to let you get&nbsp;
all the credit, take all the reward!"

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.560
If you're a fan of sci-fi movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.620
Roy Batty's words in the rain are&nbsp;
probably very familiar to you.

00:01:04.815 --> 00:01:10.785
"I've seen things you people wouldn't believe."

00:01:10.785 --> 00:01:15.420
Spoken by Rutger Hauer's character at the end&nbsp;
of Ridley Scott's magnum opus "Blade Runner,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:19.860
this brief speech might be the most quoted and&nbsp;
lauded monologue in the history of the genre.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:25.620
In just 42 words, Batty sums up so much of the&nbsp;
film's complexity. Rick Deckard has spent the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:30.300
entire film hunting Batty and his fellow rogue&nbsp;
"replicant" androids. As a blade runner, that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:34.800
his job. But as he and the viewer become more&nbsp;
confused as to the nature of his own humanity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:40.140
and as the clear injustice of replicant life&nbsp;
becomes clearer, the story blurs. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:44.820
Batty saves Rick's life, and he chooses to&nbsp;
spend his final moments sharing some of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:49.620
unique experiences. There's an intangible&nbsp;
blend of joy, sorrow, and wistfulness that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:54.240
Hauer delivers, poetically conveying what&nbsp;
philosophical exposition couldn't possibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:59.160
manage to do. Any life is a beautiful&nbsp;
thing, and any death is a tragic one.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:06.400
"Like tears in rain."

00:02:07.331 --> 00:02:12.180
The ending of Alex Garland's critically acclaimed&nbsp;
2018 film "Annihilation" is one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:17.460
beautiful, baffling, unnerving, and memorable&nbsp;
moments in recent sci-fi history. Biologist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:21.720
Lena spends the film journeying through a patch&nbsp;
of wilderness surrounded by a strange energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:27.540
field known as the Shimmer, which has been slowly&nbsp;
emanating outward from a crashed meteor. Within,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:31.920
the genes of all life have been scrambled in&nbsp;
gorgeous and hideous ways — but that's nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:36.540
compared to what Lena finds in the lighthouse at&nbsp;
the center of the Shimmer. Lena discovers her lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:41.040
squad leader, Dr. Ventress, in the tower,&nbsp;
who gives her a dark and cryptic warning.

00:02:41.357 --> 00:02:48.377
"Our bodies and our minds will be fragmented into&nbsp;
their smallest parts, until not one part remains."

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:54.000
Ventress evaporates and becomes light. The light&nbsp;
becomes a body, and the body becomes Lena. Under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.280
the haunting strangeness of Ben Salisbury and&nbsp;
Geoff Barrow's musical score, Lena and her alien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.140
double engage in a dancelike duel with violent&nbsp;
ends. It's cosmic horror at its truest and most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:09.420
powerful — a vision of alien life so different&nbsp;
from ours that it defies explanation. Many have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:13.865
tried to explain the ending of "Annihilation,"&nbsp;
but the mystery is why it's so flawless.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:19.920
"Interstellar" isn't necessarily the most original&nbsp;
sci-fi film of the 21st century, but that hasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.300
stopped it from becoming one of the most beloved.&nbsp;
In style and tone, the film is very similar to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:28.260
the rest of Christopher Nolan's filmography.&nbsp;
Thematically, it reads like a modern version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:33.240
of "2001: A Space Odyssey" — a movie that's&nbsp;
fascinated with both the theoretical science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:39.060
and strangeness of space. However, "Interstellar"&nbsp;
shines brightest in its most human moments — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:43.080
if you've seen the movie, one scene in&nbsp;
particular is probably leaping to mind right now.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:47.280
In an effort to find new horizons for&nbsp;
humanity, Joseph Cooper and the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:51.420
the Endurance team are tasked with visiting&nbsp;
several different planets in orbit around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:55.860
the black hole Gargantua. Their first stop&nbsp;
is an hour-long visit to an aquatic world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.860 --> 00:04:00.660
that unfortunately brings nothing in the way of&nbsp;
scientific revelation. Due to miscalculations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:04.860
of the black hole's time dilation and an&nbsp;
unforeseen tragedy on the planet's surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:09.540
Cooper and Dr. Amelia Brand return to the&nbsp;
Endurance to find that 23 years have passed.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:13.560
This moment is designed to wreck you.&nbsp;
Before the mission even really gets started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:18.060
the crew is broken. As Cooper watches&nbsp;
two decades of backlogged video messages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:22.860
from his now-grown children, he completely&nbsp;
breaks down. The music from Hans Zimmer — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:26.580
composer famous for sweeping anthems&nbsp;
and soaring strings — is quiet here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:30.991
as if it too has been hushed by the&nbsp;
terrifying vastness and power of space.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:35.220
It's not every year that we get a new&nbsp;
film worthy of the sci-fi cinema pantheon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:39.780
but 2022 brought an instant classic with&nbsp;
"Everything Everywhere All at Once." The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:44.760
hilarious, emotional, and infinitely creative&nbsp;
family drama from directors Daniel Kwan and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:48.360
Daniel Scheinert is the rare film&nbsp;
that occupies a space all its own.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:53.220
Even in the modern crowd of multiverse movies,&nbsp;
"Everything Everywhere All at Once" stands out.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:57.840
The movie is a non-stop rollercoaster of&nbsp;
bizarre alternate realities and exciting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:02.400
martial arts action, all anchored by a powerful&nbsp;
story about the things we give up for the people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:07.260
we love. In the third act, though, things really&nbsp;
kick into high gear. Protagonist Evelyn fully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:11.580
locks into the multiverse she's been accessing&nbsp;
and starts racing through it at a fever pitch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:15.600
leading to a stunning montage of different&nbsp;
realities flashing by her in an instant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:20.160
It's a perfect encapsulation of the film's&nbsp;
unique style, and a moment that leaves a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:24.900
lasting impression. Everything from animated&nbsp;
universes to celestial landscapes and snowy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:29.640
city streets flash by while the camera stays&nbsp;
rooted firmly on Evelyn's face. We see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:33.720
infinite possibilities of her life, and we&nbsp;
feel the weight of the decisions she's made.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:39.060
Though the montage itself only lasts a moment,&nbsp;
it leads to an equally frenetic final act with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:43.440
plenty of other wild scenes. "Everything&nbsp;
Everywhere All at Once" beautifully balances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:47.520
chaos with grounded emotion, and it all&nbsp;
comes together flawlessly at the end.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:53.280
In 2010, hot off the star-making success of&nbsp;
"The Dark Knight," Christopher Nolan dropped an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:58.500
original sci-fi masterpiece that took the world by&nbsp;
storm. "Inception" was a success on many levels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:03.120
achieving high marks from critics and raking&nbsp;
in cash at the box office with a star-studded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:07.440
cast. It also introduced a sound effect that&nbsp;
has haunted blockbuster trailers ever since.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:12.660
Because the dream-sharing technology at the center&nbsp;
of "Inception" is so high-concept, it's easy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:17.100
forget that the film is still, at its heart, a&nbsp;
heist movie. But when you hit the climax, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:21.960
becomes pretty clear. After diving down into the&nbsp;
forbidden third level of target Robert Fischer's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:26.880
dream world, the crew prepares for extraction.&nbsp;
To get out, they trigger "kicks" — weightless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:32.280
moments that wake dreamers — in all three active&nbsp;
dream levels at once. This causes time, gravity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:36.360
and the very fabric of "reality" to unravel&nbsp;
in different ways across the different layers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:42.317
resulting in one of the most intense, unique, and&nbsp;
impressively shot sci-fi action sequences ever.

00:06:43.169 --> 00:06:45.420
Calling "2001: A Space Odyssey"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:50.400
influential would be a dramatic understatement.&nbsp;
Stanley Kubrick and Arthur C. Clarke's 1968&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:55.020
sci-fi epic basically defined an entire era&nbsp;
for the genre, and it's still one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:59.400
talked-about movies today. In truth, any number&nbsp;
of scenes could be plucked from the film for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:03.600
this list. We could talk about the infamous&nbsp;
Star Child ending, or the bizarre monolith&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:07.701
sequences. But instead, let's talk about&nbsp;
the scene that everybody loves to quote.

00:07:07.701 --> 00:07:09.561
"Open the pod bay doors, HAL."

00:07:09.561 --> 00:07:13.560
The iconic line itself is recognizable&nbsp;
to many people who've never even seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:16.980
the movie. It's the moment that our&nbsp;
protagonist, astronaut Dave Bowman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:21.480
realizes that his ship's A.I. isn't just&nbsp;
sinister, but way, way ahead of him.

00:07:21.894 --> 00:07:25.314
"I know that you and Frank&nbsp;
were planning to disconnect me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.246 --> 00:07:29.066
and I'm afraid that is something&nbsp;
I can't allow to happen."

00:07:29.066 --> 00:07:33.000
"2001" is more about tone and atmosphere&nbsp;
than it is about a straightforward story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:37.800
and this scene is the ultimate example of that.&nbsp;
The unsettling sound of HAL's mechanical voice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:40.800
intercut with shots of the empty&nbsp;
ship and the dark void of space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:43.740
creates a sense of instant, unstoppable dread.

00:07:43.901 --> 00:07:48.641
"Although you took very careful precautions&nbsp;
in the pod, against my hearing you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.777 --> 00:07:51.397
I could see your lips move."

00:07:51.397 --> 00:07:56.040
Dave's own shots are claustrophobic, and&nbsp;
we see the fear play out on his face as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.040 --> 00:08:00.120
HAL shows his hand. In an age when A.I.&nbsp;
technology is becoming a bigger part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:04.800
our lives every day, this scene is even&nbsp;
more foreboding now than it was in 1968.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:10.860
Many of Steven Spielberg's most famous films&nbsp;
fall into the sci-fi genre, including 1977's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:15.720
"Close Encounters of the Third Kind" — Spielberg's&nbsp;
first movie after breaking out as a blockbuster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:21.660
director with "Jaws" in 1975. "Close Encounters"&nbsp;
is a movie all about aliens, but it plays out more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:25.800
like a mystery than a straightforward drama&nbsp;
or action movie. In fact, there's very little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:30.900
action to speak of at all, even when humanity&nbsp;
finally makes contact with the extraterrestrials.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:36.180
The ending of "Close Encounters" is as beautiful&nbsp;
as it is mystifying. The aliens finally touch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:40.800
down in Wyoming, where the U.S. military has&nbsp;
laid out a welcome party. To communicate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:45.420
the humans use a combination of light patterns&nbsp;
on a giant screen and musical notes inspired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:49.680
by the aliens' own strange sounds. In this&nbsp;
way, the two species are able to communicate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.680
though it's still a bit unclear&nbsp;
what the conversation entails.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:56.880
But then again, that's kind of the point. The&nbsp;
audiovisual communication with the aliens is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:01.140
tonally perfect, maintaining the alluring&nbsp;
mystique of the rest of the film while also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:05.700
offering a satisfying resolution. We don't get&nbsp;
concrete answers about what the aliens have done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:10.440
or what they want, but we do get a powerful,&nbsp;
resonant moment of connection between two corners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:15.134
of the universe. Even decades later,&nbsp;
the scene is absolutely breathtaking.

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:20.460
A lot of the best moments in sci-fi movie history&nbsp;
are scenes that challenge viewers intellectually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:25.380
giving form to impossible things or exploring&nbsp;
mind-blowing ideas. Some, though, are great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:29.520
simply because it's fun to see big, beefy&nbsp;
dudes fight aliens with knives in the jungle.

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:32.640
"Predator" may not be the most&nbsp;
thought-provoking science fiction movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:37.260
but it's a certified classic. The first&nbsp;
action movie from director John McTiernan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:41.580
who later conquered the genre with the "Die&nbsp;
Hard" franchise, follows a squad of muscle-bound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:46.440
military commandos on a routine mission into&nbsp;
a South American jungle. Things go awry when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:50.880
they start to be hunted by an invisible&nbsp;
extraterrestrial foe: the Predator itself.

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:55.500
By the time the final act rolls around, it's&nbsp;
just Dutch and the Predator. Slathered in mud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.500 --> 00:10:00.420
to avoid the alien's thermal vision, Dutch lays&nbsp;
traps, ambushing his enemy in the same way it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:05.460
been taking out his comrades. Sparks fly, and the&nbsp;
jungle lights up with fire and explosions as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:10.000
two titans go at it. We even get a few choice&nbsp;
one-liners along the way to keep things light.

00:10:13.225 --> 00:10:15.601
"What the hell are you?"

00:10:15.601 --> 00:10:20.520
Sometimes, a great scene doesn't need to tap into&nbsp;
the deepest recesses of your mind. Sometimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:25.234
it just needs to be flashy and fun, and&nbsp;
the ending of "Predator" is exactly that.

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:30.540
There are a lot of things to love about the&nbsp;
Wachowski sisters' breakout sci-fi masterpiece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:35.520
from the stunning visual style and rip-roaring&nbsp;
action to the heady A.I. storyline. And in truth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:39.720
many different moments could justify spots&nbsp;
on this list. The red-pill-blue-pill scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:44.280
for instance, or the slow-mo lobby shootout&nbsp;
that kicks off the final act. But one moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:48.120
stands tall above the rest: When Neo&nbsp;
finally unlocks his true potential.

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:53.160
"What are you trying to tell&nbsp;
me? That I can dodge bullets?"

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:57.480
After seemingly being killed by Agent Smith&nbsp;
and his compatriots, it looks like Neo might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:01.560
not be the one after all. But a real-world&nbsp;
kiss from Trinity gives him a second wind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:06.360
and when he stands up, he's a very different man.&nbsp;
As foretold by Morpheus, Neo blocks the agents'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:10.980
bullets like they're nothing — and when Smith&nbsp;
comes in for a closer attack, he remains equally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:15.720
untouchable. Suddenly, the most dangerous forces&nbsp;
in the Matrix are reduced to frightened children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:21.060
and Neo finishes Smith off with an explosive&nbsp;
display of his reality-altering power. This moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:25.800
is only made better by the final shot of the film,&nbsp;
which sees Neo take flight for the first time.

00:11:26.795 --> 00:11:31.200
"Tron" might not have been a huge success&nbsp;
in 1982, but it's become a cult classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:35.400
since — and its historical impact can't&nbsp;
be understated. According to The Guardian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:40.740
"Tron" was one of the first feature films to use&nbsp;
CGI in a major way, which fit perfectly with its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:45.600
experimental cyber-world setting. The most famous&nbsp;
action set piece from the film today is probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:50.760
the Light Cycle battle sequence — a combat racing&nbsp;
scene that was incredibly ambitious for its time.

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:55.560
Decades ago, the original "Tron" might have made&nbsp;
it on this list with its Light Cycles. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.560 --> 00:12:00.720
the scene hasn't aged especially well — but&nbsp;
fans got a renovated version in 2010's "Tron:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:05.340
Legacy." Director of the original film and&nbsp;
a producer on the sequel, Steven Lisberger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:09.600
told Den of Geek that the Light Cycles in "Tron:&nbsp;
Legacy" looked a lot more like what they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:13.620
going for in the early '80s. The same could&nbsp;
be said of the entire Light Cycle battle, too.

00:12:14.156 --> 00:12:16.076
"Now this I can do."

00:12:16.076 --> 00:12:20.880
In "Tron: Legacy," the combat race is nearly&nbsp;
operatic, anchored and elevated by Daft Punk's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:25.500
stellar soundtrack. Every movement is timed to&nbsp;
the music, creating a scene that's more like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:30.240
cyberspace music video than a typical action set&nbsp;
piece. While it may not be the most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:34.440
moment in the movie's overall narrative, the&nbsp;
Light Cycle battle is everything it needs to be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:39.868
It's great to see a classic scene revitalized so&nbsp;
well, and the score is just the icing on the cake.

00:12:39.868 --> 00:12:41.865
"Now that's what I'm talking about!"

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:46.980
George Miller's 2015 film "Mad Max: Fury&nbsp;
Road" isn't just a sequel to his classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:51.300
trilogy from decades earlier. It's a&nbsp;
partial reboot and quasi-reimagining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:54.960
that incorporates elements from every&nbsp;
prior film. Picking a single moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:59.160
from "Fury Road" to include is tough because&nbsp;
the film is basically one long chase scene.

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:03.720
However, there probably isn't a more memorable&nbsp;
sequence than the sandstorm battle in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:08.520
first act. From the pull-out shot that shows the&nbsp;
gargantuan size of the storm to the final shot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:13.920
Nux's flare burning out into darkness, the whole&nbsp;
sequence is breathtaking. It's the perfect way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:18.534
convey the natural brutality of this world&nbsp;
and the fragility of human life within it.

00:13:19.286 --> 00:13:24.120
For movie fans of a certain age, "E.T." is the&nbsp;
quintessential alien movie. It's untouchable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:28.920
in its simplicity — the story of a young boy&nbsp;
who finds, befriends, and fights to protect a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:34.140
gentle extraterrestrial left behind by his people.&nbsp;
With Steven Spielberg's impeccable direction and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:39.060
John Williams' triumphant score, the film takes&nbsp;
this basic pitch and makes it a deeply emotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:43.320
journey. And of course, it also features&nbsp;
perhaps the most famous shot in '80s film.

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:47.280
You already know the one. With E.T. bundled&nbsp;
in a blanket on the front of his bike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:51.480
Elliott goes for an evening ride — and&nbsp;
thanks to the alien's supernatural powers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:55.920
they're able to take flight. The shot of the bike&nbsp;
crossing in front of the moon might as well be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.920 --> 00:14:01.080
the logo for Hollywood blockbusters, even decades&nbsp;
after it first dazzled audiences. Williams' music&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.080 --> 00:14:06.060
soars in the background, just as E.T. and Elliott&nbsp;
fly before our eyes. It's the kind of scene that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.060 --> 00:14:10.740
made Spielberg one of the most lauded directors of&nbsp;
all time, and it just might be his finest moment.

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:15.300
Ridley Scott's "Alien" is one of the&nbsp;
most beloved and influential films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:19.680
in the entire sci-fi genre, both for its&nbsp;
masterfully implemented horror elements and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:23.940
its dense environmental worldbuilding. Most&nbsp;
of the action takes place on the Nostromo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:28.500
a mining ship on its way back to Earth that's&nbsp;
infiltrated by a deadly alien predator called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:31.980
a Xenomorph. The whole reason that the&nbsp;
xenomorph gets on board at all, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:36.720
is that the ship receives a signal from a nearby&nbsp;
moon. Weylan-Yutani company policy demands that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:41.700
the crew investigate, leading to a gorgeously&nbsp;
unsettling expedition onto an alien spacecraft.

00:14:41.700 --> 00:14:46.560
From the stark landscapes of the moon itself&nbsp;
to H. R. Giger's designs for the alien ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:51.240
this whole sequence oozes atmosphere and&nbsp;
dread. It captures the otherworldly nature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:55.080
of what's about to happen flawlessly, and&nbsp;
things only get more unsettling when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:59.220
first "facehugger" appears. There are plenty&nbsp;
of other horrifying moments in "Alien," but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:03.240
the whole tone is set during this first-contact&nbsp;
sequence. Without directly explaining anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:07.200
the film effortlessly conveys the&nbsp;
terrifying strangeness of the universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:11.520
which is the whole point. After all,&nbsp;
in space, no one can hear you scream.

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:16.800
The "Star Wars" franchise is so important to&nbsp;
the sci-fi genre at large that it earns two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:20.640
entries on this list — and there should be&nbsp;
no doubt that "The Empire Strikes Back" is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:25.440
deserving. From the snowy battle on Hoth in the&nbsp;
first act to Yoda's Jedi teachings on Dagobah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:30.600
the film is loaded with memorable moments, but the&nbsp;
sequence we've chosen is particularly significant.

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:34.440
"Star Wars" is nothing if not loyal to&nbsp;
narrative traditions, and the showdown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:38.940
between Luke Skywalker and Darth Vader on&nbsp;
Cloud City is about as "hero's journey" as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:43.860
it gets. It's easily the best saber fight in the&nbsp;
original "Star Wars" trilogy, and it ends with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:48.600
one of cinema's most memorable plot twists. As&nbsp;
Vader holds out his hand to his son, promising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:53.468
wealth and power if they join forces, we see in&nbsp;
Luke's eyes a combination of terror and hate.

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:59.790
"Join me and together we can rule&nbsp;
the galaxy as father and son."

00:15:59.790 --> 00:16:04.740
And yet, Luke dodges the dark side, instead&nbsp;
letting himself fall rather than to join his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:08.040
father. It's a moment that's been done&nbsp;
a hundred times in a hundred stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:10.560
but it's never been more&nbsp;
iconic than in "Star Wars."

